{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.35bb276b72","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-81","passage":"<p>The market, held every Saturday in the town ______ produce from local farms.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["square sells","square—sells","square, sells","square; sells"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase “held every Saturday in the town square” is extra information about “the market.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.263c0f6ef5","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-81","passage":"<p>The market—held every Saturday in the town ______ produce from local farms.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["square, sells","square; sells","square sells","square—sells"],"answer":"D","explanation":"The phrase “held every Saturday in the town square” is extra information about “the market.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>dashes</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.a790e1044a","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-83","passage":"<p>The poet—who rarely gave public ______ to appear at the festival.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["readings—agreed","readings agreed","readings, agreed","readings; agreed"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The phrase “who rarely gave public readings” is extra information about “the poet.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>dashes</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.a8af4d0a46","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-87","passage":"<p>The dam, built to control seasonal ______ generates electricity for the region.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["flooding—also","flooding also","flooding, also","flooding; also"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase “built to control seasonal flooding” is extra information about “the dam.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.9f7127dde1","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-1","passage":"<p>The recipe requires only four ______, water, salt, and yeast.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["ingredients: flour","ingredients, flour","ingredients; flour","ingredients flour"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The recipe requires only four ingredients” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.4c52db59a9","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-7","passage":"<p>The novel explores a single, powerful ______ difficulty of forgiving someone you love.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["theme the","theme: the","theme, and the","theme; the"],"answer":"B","explanation":"“The novel explores a single, powerful theme” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.2a6c520765","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-13","passage":"<p>The survey asked residents about two ______ transportation and affordable housing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["issues, and public","issues; public","issues: public","issues public"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The survey asked residents about two issues” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.7e579270c0","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-18","passage":"<p>The museum's collection includes items from every era of the town's ______, letters, photographs, and clothing.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["history; tools","history, tools","history: tools","history tools"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The museum's collection includes items from every era of the town's history” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.c919645c5d","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-53","passage":"<p>The storm caused one major ______ power outage lasting three days.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["problem; a","problem a","problem: a","problem, and a"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The storm caused one major problem” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."}
